## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to bridge Go and C with CGO, focusing on asynchronous progress reporting, memory ownership, and safe concurrent invocation.

## Key Concepts
- CGO cross-language callbacks
- Go pointer safety and C context passing
- Memory ownership and cleanup across boundaries
- Concurrent calls and per-call isolation

## Code Example
```c
// header
typedef void (*progress_cb_t)(size_t processed, size_t total, void* ctx);
void start_processing(const unsigned char* data, size_t len, progress_cb_t cb, void* ctx);
```

```c
// minimal implementation sketch (alloc-free in this example)
#include <stddef.h>

typedef void (*progress_cb_t)(size_t, size_t, void*);
void start_processing(const unsigned char* data, size_t len, progress_cb_t cb, void* ctx) {
  // pretend to process in chunks and invoke cb once per chunk
  size_t chunks = 4, i;
  for (i = 1; i <= chunks; ++i) {
    cb((size_t)i * (len / chunks), len, ctx);
  }
}
```

```go
// Go binding sketch (cgobind.go)
package main
import "C"

type ProgressEvent struct { Processed, Total C.size_t }

//export progressCallback
func progressCallback(processed C.size_t, total C.size_t, ctx unsafe.Pointer) {
  ch := *(*chan ProgressEvent)(ctx)
  ch <- ProgressEvent{Processed: processed, Total: total}
}

func StartProcessing(data []byte) (<-chan ProgressEvent, <-chan error) {
  ch := make(chan ProgressEvent)
  errCh := make(chan error, 1)
  // pass &ch as ctx to C and start_processing
  C.start_processing((*C.uchar)(&data[0]), C.size_t(len(data)), C.progress_cb_t(C.progressCallback), unsafe.Pointer(&ch))
  return ch, errCh
}
```

